# The pinned Markdown To browser bundle.
#
# mdto.js in this directory is a VERBATIM COPY of site/app/mdto.js from the
# markdownto repository at the commit below — the same artifact markdownto.ai's
# playground serves. It is never edited here and never fetched at runtime: the
# Hub serves this exact copy from its own origin, with an `integrity` attribute
# computed from these bytes, so a rendered page is pinned to one engine version
# and an upgrade is a deliberate, reviewable commit.
#
# Re-vendoring (docs/internals/markdownto-rendering.md has the full procedure):
#
#   1. In the markdownto repo, build the bundle: node site/tools/build-app.mjs
#   2. cp site/app/mdto.js  <agentsfs>/internal/hub/assets/mdto/mdto.js
#   3. Update commit + bundle-commit + sha256 + vendored below
#      (git -C <markdownto> rev-parse HEAD, git -C <markdownto> log -1
#       --format=%H -- site/app/mdto.js, shasum -a 256 …/mdto/mdto.js)
#   4. go test ./internal/hub/ -run Mdto   (the manifest is asserted against the
#      served bytes, so a stale line here fails the build, not production)
#
# Format: `key: value`, one per line; `#` comments and blank lines ignored.
#
# `commit` is the markdownto revision this copy was taken FROM; `bundle-commit`
# is the last commit that changed these bytes. They answer different questions
# ("which tree did you vendor?" and "how old is the engine really?") and both are
# full object ids. This update preserves original HTML pages beneath guided
# narration, with sanitized presentation and capture-to-DOM targeting.
#
# What v0.3.1 adds that this Hub depends on: the guided reader accepts a
# recording the HOST hands it on `guided-restore` (`saved.recording`) and answers
# `guided-recording-ready` / `guided-recording-refused`. That is what lets a
# reader living in an opaque-origin frame with `connect-src 'none'` speak in a
# real voice — see docs/internals/markdownto-rendering.md.

